# A subscriber socket that fails every read must cost the daemon a bounded
# slice of one core, and must stay visible on the log and on the counter.
# readLoop (internal/component/l2tp/listener.go) paces its retry for that
# reason, and this scenario is what proves the pacing is real rather than
# asserted.
#
# THE STIMULUS. `ze-test fail-syscall` installs a classic seccomp filter that
# answers ENETDOWN for recvfrom, but only for a read of 1500 bytes, which is
# rxBufLen and so selects the listener's own socket and no other. It then
# execs the daemon. The kernel refuses the call at its entry, so the failing
# read is charged to the daemon's own CPU time, which is what the fixture
# measures off /metrics. The launcher refuses to start anything if it did not
# arm: it makes the selected call once itself, and a second call of a length
# it must NOT select, before the exec.
#
# WHY NOT ptrace. `strace -e inject=recvfrom:error=ENETDOWN:when=1+` sustains
# the same error and destroys the reading with it. Go's runtime emits a
# continuous SIGURG stream for goroutine preemption, ptrace traps every
# delivery regardless of --seccomp-bpf, and time spent ptrace-stopped counts
# as wall clock rather than as the tracee's CPU. Measured on 2026-09-11: by
# hand the pre-fix build reached 36,640 read errors and 0.99 CPU-seconds,
# and through this .ci the SAME pre-fix build reported 19 errors and no
# measurable CPU delta, passing every assertion below. That vacuous version
# lived in test/draft/l2tp/ until the seccomp mechanism replaced it
# (spec-failing-socket-proof-needs-a-non-ptrace-injection-point).
#
# LD_PRELOAD does not apply: ze builds CGO_ENABLED=0 and makes raw syscalls,
# so there is no libc call to interpose on.
#
# WHAT IT DISCRIMINATES. Measured in the QEMU guest on ze's runtime kernel,
# 2026-09-11, over the same 3s window under the same armed filter:
#   paced (this tree): 12 read errors, 0.0133 of one core
#   pacer reverted:    1,053,776 read errors, 2.1067 of one core
# The fixture's bounds (1000 errors, 0.5 of a core) sit between them, with
# three orders of magnitude of headroom on the counter.
#
# DO NOT DELETE THE expect=stderr LINE. It reads like a third copy of what the
# two stdout lines already assert, and it is not: the fixture reads /metrics
# and nothing else, so a daemon that serves metrics while writing no log at
# all passes both stdout assertions. That daemon is exactly what this scenario
# produced on 2026-09-11. `ze-test fail-syscall` execs the daemon, and every
# cmd/ze binary starts by dup2-ing a crashlog pipe over fd 2 whose draining
# goroutine the exec destroys, so the launched daemon wrote into a pipe nobody
# read and blocked once 64 KiB filled it. The launcher now calls
# crashlog.Flush() before the exec (internal/test/failsyscall, Run), and this
# line is the only assertion in the tree that would go red if that call were
# removed (plan/journal/output-lost-to-an-exit-past-the-flush.md).
option=needs-linux

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
l2tp {
	enabled true
	shared-secret s3cr3t
}
environment {
	l2tp {
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT
		}
	}
}

telemetry {
	prometheus {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT2
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

option=env:var=ze.log.l2tp:value=debug
option=env:var=ze.l2tp.skip-kernel-probe:value=true
# The runner adds this one itself when the launched binary IS ze. Here the
# launched binary is the fault-injection launcher, so the test states it:
# a functional daemon keeps its state out of the developer's shared zefs.
option=env:var=ze.storage.blob:value=false

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-test fail-syscall syscall recvfrom errno ENETDOWN length 1500 -- ze -:stdin=config
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture l2tp/failing-socket $PORT2:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=OK: CPU use stayed at
expect=stdout:contains=OK: ze_l2tp_listener_read_errors_total rose by
expect=stderr:contains=l2tp: listener read error
